        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 274,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:50:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64005,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 274,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EPS reaction occurs in the uterus, fallopian tube, and (rarely) at the sites of extrauterine gestation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:50:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64006,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 274,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most women are asymptomatic; a few present with vaginal bleeding. EPS reaction is associated with normal gestation or hydatidiform mole {11192071}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:50:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64007,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 274,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The epidemiology is unknown.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:50:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64008,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 274,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:50:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64009,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 274,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EPS reaction represents the extreme end of a physiological process rather than a true neoplasm {11192071}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:50:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64010,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 274,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>No macroscopic lesion is seen {30407075}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:50:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64011,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 274,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EPS reaction is an infiltrative lesion consisting of atypical and pleomorphic trophoblastic cells not forming a mass. The cells are mainly mononuclear, with a few interspersed multinucleated cells that are characteristically distributed evenly within the lesion. The cells are arranged in cords and small nests and display abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm, large hyperchromatic nuclei, and prominent nucleoli. The lesional cells are diffusely positive for cytokeratins and trophoblastic markers.</p>\r\n<p>The infiltrative pattern, cytological atypia, and vascular invasion may mimic placental site trophoblastic tumour. However, EPS reaction is distinguished from placental site trophoblastic tumour by the absence of a macroscopic lesion, the lack of confluent/sheet-like growth, preserved endomyometrial architecture, lack of mitoses, presence of chorionic villi, and low Ki-67 proliferation index (&lt;&nbsp;2%). EPS reaction is well described in association with complete hydatidiform mole, and such cases may have greater atypia and a slightly higher Ki-67 proliferation index (~5&ndash;10%) than their non-molar counterparts. In this context, EPS reaction should not be interpreted as a placental site trophoblastic tumour {9445130; 11192071}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:50:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64012,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 274,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:50:29"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
